  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA cold front will sag south into the area tonight bringing\nincreasing showers and thunderstorms. A weak wave of low\npressure also moves along the front, enhancing rainfall at times.\nAhead of the front, temperatures have soared to well above \nnormal values and southwesterly low level winds have pumped\nample moisture northward with PWATS approaching 2 inches along\nthe front. All of this points to a potential for a few severe\nthunderstorms later this afternoon, with gusty winds the primary\nthreat, and then heavy rainfall overnight as the front stalls \nout bisecting the area in a west to east orientation. This sets\nup the potential for repetitive rounds of showers and storms \nwith WPC highlighting a large chunk of our CWA under a Slight \nRisk for excessive rainfall overnight.\n\nThe front lifts north as a warm front tomorrow resulting in a\nlull in precipitation during the afternoon and another warm day.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nForecast models continue to slow down the frontal passage on\nFriday with the general consensus now being a midday or\nafternoon passage. This should be accompanied by a broken line \nof showers, with just enough instability for perhaps a \nthunderstorm or two. The bigger concern is gusty winds though \nwith potential for widespread 30 to 40 mph winds and higher in \nthe higher elevations. This will cause scattered power outages \nif it materializes.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nBreezy north winds continue to usher in a seasonably cool\nairmass Saturday with a few light rain showers, and maybe some\nmountain flurries in the morning. Winds then die down Saturday \nnight as a sprawling area of high pressure builds in and this \nwill support temperatures falling to around freezing for many \nlocations and frost/freeze headlines will likely be needed. \nTemperatures then slowly moderate into next week but at least \nsome patchy frost will again be possible Sunday night into \nMonday morning.",
